  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A pig ate a man's potatoes and nearly started a war. San Juan Island, June 1859. One shot, one dead pig, two empires.",
        "visual_query": "pacific northwest island farmland coast",
        "visual_fallback": "island farmland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The farmer was American. The pig worked for the Hudson's Bay Company. Both countries claimed the island, and neither government would back down an inch.",
        "visual_query": "19th century frontier farm buildings",
        "visual_fallback": "frontier farm"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Two months later: four hundred sixty-one American soldiers and fourteen cannons against five Royal Navy warships, seventy guns, two thousand one hundred and forty men.",
        "visual_query": "19th century naval sailing warship",
        "visual_fallback": "sailing warship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "All of it over a pig. The British admiral flatly refused to fire, saying he would not go to war over a squabble about a pig.",
        "visual_query": "british royal navy harbour historic",
        "visual_fallback": "naval harbour"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So they shared the island instead, for thirteen years, until an arbitration ruling handed it to America in 1872. Total casualties: the pig.",
        "visual_query": "san juan islands coastline aerial",
        "visual_fallback": "island coastline"
      }
    ],
